  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nHeading into Sunday evening, showers and storms from earlier in the \nday begin to decay and move to the east. During the day on Monday, a \ncold front approaches from the north, but with the onset of daytime \nheating, it will slow and stall between Midland and Lubbock. Rain \nchances increase along and behind the front Monday evening. The \nfront pushes south into the region on Tuesday and rain chances \nspread nearly areawide with the highest chances (50-70%) across \nsoutheast New Mexico and the Permian Basin. Lower chances (20-40%) \ncontinue south of the I-10 corridor. \n\nEach day from Tuesday through Thursday will be similar with rounds \nof rain developing each of those afternoons mainly across the \nnorthern half of the CWA. Low confidence on how much will fall over \nthe course of the week and will depend on where the most favorable \nenvironment will be each day. Medium range continues to put the \nfocus over southeast New Mexico and into central New Mexico. Highs \nduring this time drop into the 80s to low 90s for most, which is an \nimprovement from the late season heat, but will still be above \nnormal for the end of September. Overnight lows settle into the 60s \npartly because of a cooler airmass and northeasterly flow at the \nsurface as well as increased cloud cover and rain chances. \n\n-Stickney",
